UP|52|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|The @S10 is a reasonable lead and turns out to be the best lead, though I would have led a heart which works out badly. Many play ^*BCoded Tens and Nines^*n particularly in NT. Prior to coded tens and nines, players led the top of an interior sequence in NT as they would lead Jack from KJ10, or AJ10, or 10 from A109 K109, or Q109.|
PG| ***** 75 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Against NT, when you lead suit with an interior sequence headed by the J (AJ10 or KJ10) the standard lead is the Jack. However you also lead the Jack from J109 and J108 when you don't hold a higher honor.|
PG| ***** 76 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|The ^*BCoded 10^*n lead was invented to get rid of this ambiguity. You lead the 10 from KJ10 and AJ10. So when partner leads the jack you know he has nothing higher. You also lead the 10 from 109 with no higher honor. |
PG| ***** 77 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Having said that, when partner leads the 10, you can usually tell whether or not he has the jack and a higher honor - if the jack is not in your hand or dummy, there is a good chance partner holds it.|
PG| ***** 78 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Some people alert a coded ten lead as ^*BZero or Two Higher^*n. Although that is true, it is not a sufficient explanation because if the leader holds two higher cards, one of them is the jack and the other is never the queen.|
PG| ***** 79 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Those who play coded tens must also play coded nines. If you play standard leads, then you lead the ten from a broken sequence headed by the ten (A109, K109, or Q109). But playing coded tens, you must lead the nine from A109, K109, or Q109 because the lead of the ten shows either nothing higher or AJ10 or KJ10.|
PG| ***** 80 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Many play that coded tens and nines only apply when leading against a NT contract. And that is fine because you rarely lead from a broken sequence against a suit contract. But there is nothing wrong with playing coded tens and nines against a suit contract to cover the rare case when you do lead from a holding with a broken sequence. |
PG| ***** 81 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Just make sure you and your partner have an agreement as to whether or not coded tens and nines apply only to NT contracts or to suit contracts as well.

|
PG| ***** 82 ***** |


