https://tinyurl.com/28yxnr6b
qx|A6|st||rh||ah|Board 5 IMPs   -   On November 27, 2024|pn|hondo717,Honeydog3,marikaSz,danpsych|
md|4SKQ98432H6D6C7543,SAJ76HAJ85DJ98C92,STHQ743DKQ754CK86,S5HKT92DAT32CAQJT|sv|e|
mb|1C|mb|4S|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Let me discuss preempting at ^*BFavorable Vulnerability^*n which I hope it can explain my ultra-aggressive preempting 4@S bid. ^*BFavorable Vulnerability^*n means the vulnerability status that most favors preempting. This is when you are not vulnerable and they are vulnerable. |
PG| ***** 84 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|You want to be particularly aggressive when at ^*BFavorable Vulnerability^*n because the penalties are less. So here, although most players would bid 3@S, as would I at any vulnerability except favorable, you should stretch.|
PG| ***** 85 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|To add on my aggressive preempting I don't have an 8 card suit, which is what I would normally need to bid 4@S. On the plus side, my hand has great distribution with 2 singletons. With all the above considered, I am going to bid 4@S anyway. And as you shall see isn't going to work out so well here, but often this type of aggressive preempting does.|
PG| ***** 86 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|
mb|D|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|The double is not pure penalty. It is ^*BCard Showing^*n meaning East, although they often pass, can pull with spade shortness and a long suit. Here East is happy to pass. But for example if they had a 6 card club suit and a singleton or void in spades they might bid 5@C.|
PG| ***** 87 ***** |


